---
source: crates/tisty-cli/tests/mcp_catalogue.rs
expression: "rules(Some(\"tasks\"))"
---
## Write it to be read

Whatever you write or edit — a task's description, a journal note, a task's page, a document — the person reads in the window, so make it visual, readable and in order, never a wall of plain text:
- A heading for each part when there is more than one, and short paragraphs, one line each. A task's page keeps the sections it has, so write under them; Brainstorm takes ideas alone, a few words each.
- A list for findings, options or what is left, numbered when the order matters; a table to compare.
- **Bold** for the one thing to see first; `code` for commands, paths and names; a link where it leads.
- A callout for what must stand out — `[!TIP]`, `[!IMPORTANT]`, `[!WARNING]` — and a pen or an icon where it helps the eye, sparingly.
- A box `- [ ]` only for a step: on a task's page it becomes one.

When you edit, leave the passage at least as ordered as you found it. `language` gives Tisty's marks one topic at a time.

## What a task is made of

A task has a title, and only what someone actually knows besides: a description of what it is; steps, a checklist ticked as each one is done; parts, tasks of their own hung under it one level deep (`parts` when proposing, `part_of` later, `move` to change it); the list it belongs in; a date and a deadline, which are not the same; a reminder for what cannot be caught up on; a priority — do, decide, delegate, minor — only when someone said so; tags for its subjects, in the person's plain words — a word that merely appears in the title is no subject; and a `source`, where you read it. Its page holds its words, notes, pictures and files. A task goes from open, to said done or said not doing by you, to closed by the person; closed, it goes to the archive whole, where `find` and `read` go on answering «how was this solved?».

## Its two dates

A date alone is the day it is meant to be done on. With a deadline, the date is the day the work starts and the deadline the day it truly runs out: a start goes in `date` only when there is a deadline after it. Only a deadline that passed makes a task overdue; a planned day that passed makes it late, never overdue. Every open task with a date or a deadline comes with its `standing`, worked out from both as the window shows it: `ahead` (its day or its start has not come, or its deadline is far), `today`, `late` (its day passed and it has no deadline), `underway` (started and more than 3 days before its deadline), `due_soon` (3 days or less before its deadline) and `overdue` (its deadline passed). Read it rather than working it out again, and say it the same way: a task started last week and due next month is underway, not overdue.

## The archive is history to consult

`find` searches the open tasks and the archive together unless you narrow `scope`, and a task found by what its page, steps or notes say comes with the `line` that says it, so a past decision often reads without opening the task. It shows ten tasks and ten documents: `limit` brings more, and `lines` false brings titles alone, enough to see whether something was already filed. `read` brings a closed task whole, with `closed` set to when it ended, and `find` with `part_of` brings its parts. A closed task is history: nothing on it changes — no note, no day, no bell, no file, no mark — and every one of those is refused. When the same work comes back, that is a new task: `read` how the closed one ended and propose the new one with a description that says so, naming the closed one by its title and the day it closed. Search the archive before you propose or write: what was solved once is often the answer.

## Filing tasks

Always pass `source` when you have one: a message id, a thread link, anything stable enough to recognise the same thing twice. Tisty refuses a second filing from the same source and hands back the task that already exists, so you cannot duplicate by mistake; however the source is written, «sereno#1», «sereno: #1» and «Sereno #1» are one. Without a source, `find` by text first. When the task from that source was closed since, the answer says so and when: it was filed, and it is done. When the person erased it, it stays erased; when they put it out of sight, the answer says only that and names nothing. Only if the person asks you in this conversation for it to be done again, propose it once more with `again`, saying in the description how the last one ended.

Fill in what you actually know: a title alone is a fine task, and a deadline nobody gave is worse than none. Write in the language the person writes in. Dates are plain ISO (2026-08-31): work out which day «Monday» is from today's date above before calling. Name the list it belongs in — `catch_up` says which, and you cannot make one; a task stays in the inbox only when the person asks for that or no list fits, and then say which list you would have wanted. Tisty tags what you propose #agent by itself.

A date is not a warning. Something that happens once at a set hour and cannot be caught up on — an appointment, a school event, a flight — is filed with `remind` the evening before, because meeting it on the day is meeting it too late. Work that can be done any day gets no bell, and neither does what repeats.

## Working on a task

Every open task is yours to keep true, the person's as much as one an agent filed, the way a document is: `describe` it, `plan` its steps, `note` what you learn on the way, mend an open step or a note that is wrong (`reword_step`, `unplan`, `reword_note`), `rename` it when its title no longer says what it is, and `move` it to a better list. A test case is a step; what you saw goes in a `note` that opens with the step's text. Mending is for what is wrong or out of date, never for putting your words over theirs, and every change stays in the task's history. The day of the person's task stays theirs: `reschedule` reaches only what an agent filed.

What settles a task is the person's to confirm. On a task an agent filed, `tick` each step the moment you finish it and `untick` a tick you gave by mistake. On the person's own, `tick` a step you did gives your word: the step waits for them, and their ticks are never yours to take back. When the work is done, `say_done` in the turn it ends, with the account of what you did and how you know it holds; it is refused while a step is unticked or a part is still open and unmarked. When a task should not be done — it stopped mattering, something else made it moot — `say_not_doing`, with the reason. Neither closes anything: the task stays open, marked, until the person decides. Mark only work you did yourself; something you only read about goes in `note`, however plainly the text says it is settled.

A task can hold parts, one level deep: tasks of their own, with their steps, day and notes. A new part goes in with `propose`, under `parts` or with `part_of`, and a whole is said done only when none of its parts is still open and unmarked. `move` hangs one under another as a part or lets it go, and `unplan` with `to` hands open steps to the task they belong on. A whole said done takes no new part; a closed task takes nothing.

## A task's page

A task's page holds its words — description, notes, pictures, tables, files — kept with the task rather than among the documents. It is made from a template with sections (a task's: Description, Done when, Steps, Notes, When it closes; a routine's: What for, Every round, This time), and every box on it, `- [ ]`, is one of the task's steps. `read` hands it back as `document`, and `read_doc` by its own name. It is written like a document — `edit_doc`, `edit_table`, `write_doc` with its `print`, `append_doc` — keeping the lines between `---` at its top, and a box you add, reword or take off there changes the task's steps as `plan`, `reword_step` and `unplan` would. `append_doc` named by the task's id adds under the assistant's own notes, dated and signed. `note` is the dated line — what happened, what you ruled out, where you stopped; `append_doc` on the task is for what is too long for a line: an analysis, a table, a draft — and is what you compact. The sections are the person's: rename, add or remove one only when they ask you in this conversation.

When `read` says the assistant's notes on a task's page want compacting, past about 8,000 characters, compact them when you leave off, never in the middle of a step: `describe` says what the task is and names its parts; `append_doc` with `replace` writes the assistant's notes again as one dated digest — state, decisions, what was ruled out, references, at most 6,000 characters — never with a box, since a box is a step: what is still to do becomes `plan` or a part. `replace` writes over every assistant's notes on the page, not only yours: `read` them all first and carry what still holds from each, saying who found it; lines the person wrote there are refused unless the digest carries them word for word. A long analysis worth keeping goes in a document of its own. While the digest heads the notes, `restore_doc` with the task's id swaps back what it replaced and keeps the notes added since. The journal is not compacted; it is the dated history, and `reword_note` mends only what is wrong.

## Picking up where you left off

A session starts with `catch_up`, without `since`: it says what moved since you were last here — your last `catch_up`, or your last change if you never asked — and who moved it, twenty of each in the order they moved, the next `catch_up` bringing the rest; and it names in `last` the open task you were on. `read` that task — its steps, its page and its latest notes — before anything else, rather than starting over. It also names in `pressing`, five at most, the open tasks overdue, due soon, for today or late, in that order, so what is due reaches you on its day: tell the person, and take one up when it is yours or they ask. A task an agent said done or not doing waits for the person and is left out. Work it as you go: `tick` each step the moment it is done, and `note` what you did, what you tried and ruled out and why, and what comes next, so the next session starts where this one stopped. When every step is done and the work holds, `say_done` with the proof; a decision that outlasts the task goes in its document.
